{"parentUuid":"65ab4119-90c2-4429-9a0a-ab7db7262b8d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a007c0e7513c91e75","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01EtXzL7hsbzRxD5b5oKHrps","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PzxJmVzXv2SY4CpXrpStQu","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"win-win\" treewidth bidimensionality grid minor packing algorithm"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4081,"cache_read_input_tokens":24669,"output_tokens":235,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4081},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":235,"cache_read_input_tokens":24669,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4081,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4081,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaozbG8cXKfV3zYSHiQGm","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"45bf173e-5026-4fa1-b87f-ba8f03a617a3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:41.983Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"65ab4119-90c2-4429-9a0a-ab7db7262b8d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a007c0e7513c91e75","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RsCktis4e7TasVFBENicYc","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"facility placement layout CP-SAT treewidth parameterized 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Rostering: Complete Guide to Constraint Programming for Workforce Scheduling - Interactive | Michael Brenndoerfer | Michael Brenndoerfer\",\"url\":\"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/cp-sat-rostering-constraint-programming-workforce-scheduling\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint Satisfaction with Bounded Treewidth Revisited ⋆\",\"url\":\"https://www.ac.tuwien.ac.at/files/pub/SamerSzeider10a.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Steiner Tree Parameterized by Multiway Cut and Even Less\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2024.76\"},{\"title\":\"New width parameters for SAT and #SAT - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0004370221000114\"},{\"title\":\"Enumeration of Minimal Hitting Sets Parameterized by Treewidth\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol328-icdt2025/LIPIcs.ICDT.2025.8/LIPIcs.ICDT.2025.8.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint satisfaction with bounded treewidth revisited - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022000009000300\"},{\"title\":\"Some Remarks on the Incompressibility of Width-Parameterized SAT Instances | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-29700-7_18\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"}]\n\nI found the search results, but they don't contain information specifically about facility placement layout optimization using CP-SAT with treewidth parameterization from 2024-2025. \n\nThe search results cover:\n- CP-SAT as a constraint programming solver from Google OR-Tools (general applications)\n- Treewidth as a graph parameter and its use in parameterized complexity (theoretical computer science)\n- SAT and constraint satisfaction parameterized by treewidth\n- Various parameterized algorithms from 2024\n\nHowever, there don't appear to be papers or resources in these results specifically addressing \"facility placement layout\" optimization using CP-SAT with treewidth-based parameterization from 2024-2025. The search returned general information about CP-SAT and treewidth separately, but not their combination for facility placement problems.\n\nIf you're looking for academic papers on this specific topic, you might want to:\n1. Search arxiv.org directly for recent preprints\n2. Look at conference proceedings from ICML, IJCAI, or AAAI from 2024-2025\n3. Search academic databases like Google Scholar with more specific author or institution names if you have them\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"339d7c19-46c6-41fb-a1c7-ea7ed5ef8b18","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:45.712Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"65ab4119-90c2-4429-9a0a-ab7db7262b8d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"244ce4bb-dbe7-4663-9e61-eb0035d7440a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a007c0e7513c91e75","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0128EwSXJ4CRTJgWeyo1CkXK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"empty rectangle\" \"maximum\" placement obstacles algorithm grid combinatorial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Largest empty rectangle - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Largest_empty_rectangle\"},{\"title\":\"Parametric Algorithm to Find the Largest Empty Rectangle from a Set of Line Segments | International Journal of Foundations of Computer Science\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldscientific.com/doi/10.1142/S0129054124500175\"},{\"title\":\"Location of the largest empty rectangle among arbitrary obstacles | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/3-540-58715-2_122.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"1293. Shortest Path in a Grid with Obstacles Elimination - In-Depth Explanation\",\"url\":\"https://algo.monster/liteproblems/1293\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - til117/obstacles: The program constructs obstacles in an empty grid, in order for a path planning algorithm to choose a collision-free path from a starting point to the ending point of that grid.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/til117/obstacles\"},{\"title\":\"Largest empty rectangle - HandWiki\",\"url\":\"https://handwiki.org/wiki/Largest_empty_rectangle\"},{\"title\":\"Rectangles packing grid - Classiq\",\"url\":\"https://docs.classiq.io/latest/explore/applications/optimization/rectangles_packing/rectangles_packing_grid/\"},{\"title\":\"Largest empty rectangle among a point set - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0196677402002857\"},{\"title\":\"Unique paths in a Grid with Obstacles - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/unique-paths-in-a-grid-with-obstacles/\"},{\"title\":\"Computing the Largest Empty Rectangle\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~chazelle/pubs/ComputLargestEmptyRectangle.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the maximum empty rectangle placement algorithm with obstacles on a grid:\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nA maximal empty rectangle is a rectangle which is not contained in another empty rectangle, with each side of a maximal empty rectangle abutting an obstacle.\n\n## The Classic Problem\n\nA problem first discussed by Naamad, Lee and Hsu in 1983 involves finding a largest-area rectangle with sides parallel to those of a container which lies within it and does not contain any given obstacle points.\n\n## Combinatorial Approach\n\nA combinatorial algorithm to locate the Maximum Empty Rectangle (MER) inside a given set of non-intersecting horizontal and vertical line segments has been presented. A more general problem involves locating an empty isothetic rectangle of maximum area given arbitrarily-oriented non-intersecting line segments, with a novel algorithm of time complexity O(n log² n) and space complexity O(n) based on matrix searching.\n\n## Algorithm Complexity\n\nNaamad, Lee and Hsu presented an algorithm of time complexity O(min(n², s log n)) where s is the number of feasible solutions (maximal empty rectangles), and they proved that s=O(n²).\n\n## Applications and Extensions\n\nApplications include enumeration of \"maximal white rectangles\" in image segmentation for image processing and pattern recognition. The technique has been extended to locate the largest isothetic maximal empty rectangle inside arbitrary simple polygons and amidst arbitrary polygonal obstacles.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f408044a-823b-40f4-ac72-768145b52889","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:48.295Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"244ce4bb-dbe7-4663-9e61-eb0035d7440a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"45bf173e-5026-4fa1-b87f-ba8f03a617a3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a007c0e7513c91e75","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PzxJmVzXv2SY4CpXrpStQu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"win-win\" treewidth bidimensionality grid minor packing algorithm\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Fast Algorithms for Hard Graph Problems: Bidimensionality, Minors, and Local Treewidth | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-31843-9_57\"},{\"title\":\"Linearity of grid minors in treewidth with applications through bidimensionality | Combinatorica | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00493-008-2140-4\"},{\"title\":\"Bidimensionality - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bidimensionality\"},{\"title\":\"Bidimensionality | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-642-27848-8_47-2\"},{\"title\":\"[1005.5449] Bidimensionality and EPTAS\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1005.5449\"},{\"title\":\"Packing Directed Cycles Quarter- and Half-Integrally1footnote 11footnote 1 An extended abstract of this manuscript appeared at European Symposium on Algorithms 2019 [17].\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1907.02494\"},{\"title\":\"Bidimensionality and Geometric Graphs Fedor V. Fomin∗ Daniel Lokshtanov†\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1107.2221\"},{\"title\":\"Decomposition of Map Graphs with Applications\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2019.60\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Bidimensionality and EPTAS - ResearchGate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/45920569_Bidimensionality_and_EPTAS\"},{\"title\":\"The Bidimensional Theory of Bounded-Genus Graphs | SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics\",\"url\":\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/040616929\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about the relationship between treewidth, bidimensionality, grid minors, and packing algorithms:\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nAny H-minor-free graph, for a fixed graph H, of treewidth w has an Ω(w) × Ω(w) grid graph as a minor. This foundational result connects treewidth to grid minors.\n\n## Bidimensionality Theory\n\nBidimensionality theory characterizes a broad range of graph problems that admit efficient approximate, fixed-parameter or kernel solutions in a broad range of graphs, including planar graphs, map graphs, bounded-genus graphs and graphs excluding any fixed minor, building on the graph minor theory of Robertson and Seymour.\n\nThe theory of bidimensionality provides general techniques for designing efficient fixed-parameter algorithms and approximation algorithms for a broad range of NP-hard graph problems, applying to graph problems where the solution value for the k×k grid graph grows with k, typically as Ω(k²), and the solution value goes down when contracting edges and optionally when deleting edges.\n\n## Applications to Packing Problems\n\nThe bidimensionality framework allows obtaining EPTASs on (apex)-minor-free graphs for all problems covered by previous frameworks, as well as for several packing problems, partial covering problems and problems that are neither closed under taking minors nor contractions.\n\nThe search results don't specifically mention a \"win-win\" algorithm or lemma by that name, but the results show how bidimensionality uses the grid minor/treewidth dichotomy as a key algorithmic tool for developing efficient algorithms across various graph problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3c79c89d-22f5-40a1-84e7-4d6ca574f416","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:50.371Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45bf173e-5026-4fa1-b87f-ba8f03a617a3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
